  "note": {
    "Μέλητος": "Meletus, who brought the formal indictment. Socrates cross-examines him at 24c–28a.",
    "Ἄνυτος": "Anytus, a prominent politician and the weightiest of the three accusers; he appears for the craftsmen and politicians (23e–24a).",
    "Λύκων": "Lycon, the third accuser, appearing on behalf of the orators (23e–24a).",
    "Σωκράτης": "Socrates, speaking in his own defence before an Athenian jury in 399 BC.",
    "Ἀθηναῖος": "Athenian. Socrates addresses the jury throughout as ὦ ἄνδρες Ἀθηναῖοι, ‘men of Athens’.",
    "Χαιρεφῶν": "Chaerephon, Socrates' friend, who asked the Delphic oracle whether anyone was wiser than Socrates (21a).",
    "Κρίτων": "Crito, Socrates' lifelong friend and fellow-demesman, present at the trial (33d) and among those offering to guarantee the fine (38b).",
    "Κριτόβουλος": "Critobulus, son of Crito, named among those present (33e).",
    "Πρόδικος": "Prodicus of Ceos, a sophist named alongside Gorgias and Hippias (19e).",
    "Ἱππίας": "Hippias of Elis, a sophist named alongside Gorgias and Prodicus (19e).",
    "Εὔηνος": "Evenus of Paros, who taught for a fee of five minae (20b).",
    "Ἀριστοφάνης": "Aristophanes, the comic poet; Socrates points to his portrayal in the Clouds (19c).",
    "Παλαμήδης": "Palamedes, condemned unjustly by the Greeks at Troy; one of the dead Socrates imagines meeting (41b).",
    "Αἰακός": "Aeacus, one of the judges of the dead, named with Minos, Rhadamanthus and Triptolemus (41a).",
    "Ὀρφεύς": "Orpheus, named among the dead Socrates would gladly meet (41a).",
    "Μουσαῖος": "Musaeus, a legendary poet named with Orpheus and Hesiod (41a).",
    "Ἡσίοδος": "Hesiod, named among the poets Socrates would question in the underworld (41a).",
    "Ὅμηρος": "Homer, named among the poets Socrates would question in the underworld (41a).",
    "Θέτις": "Thetis, mother of Achilles, whose warning Socrates quotes from the Iliad (28c).",
